00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.400
Broadly speaking, there are two ways&nbsp;
to make housing more affordable:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.240
increase supply or decrease demand.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.720
Japan, uniquely, does both.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.680
For one, demography certainly doesn’t hurt.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.480
Its population has been declining since 2010 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.400
is expected to return to 1970 levels&nbsp;
within the next twenty-five years.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.560
Fewer people means fewer&nbsp;
households means fewer homes.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.220
That’s on the demand side. But&nbsp;
supply is no less important.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.020
To put it simply: Japan builds. like. crazy.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.200
Tokyo proper, for instance, has a&nbsp;
population of about 14 million people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:21.240
New York City, a little less than 9. But&nbsp;
despite being only 1.6 times more populous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.460
Tokyo built four and a third&nbsp;
times as many houses in 2020.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:32.940
Of course, as any Californian can tell you,&nbsp;
“just build more” is easier said than done.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:37.560
Increasing supply is threatening&nbsp;
to the very parties responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.240
for its approval — neighbors, city&nbsp;
councils, and local governments.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.200
But that’s where the Japanese system excels.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:54.240
During the late 80s and early 90s, the country&nbsp;
experienced a housing bubble that rivals 2008.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.000
At one point, all the land in Japan was&nbsp;
valued four times higher than all of America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.320
despite being twenty-six times smaller in&nbsp;
land area — about the size of Montana alone.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.680
After the market came crashing down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.460
the government made changes to prevent&nbsp;
the same thing from happening again.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.560
The tiny difference that makes all&nbsp;
the difference is that, in Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.360
land policy is now mostly set at&nbsp;
the national, not local, level.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.960
Its Ministry of Land, Infrastructure,&nbsp;
Transportation, and Tourism is less like, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.840
the little-known Federal Maritime Commission,&nbsp;
and more akin to the all-powerful EPA.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.840
It, rather than partial local governments&nbsp;
or even the legislature itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.380
largely dictates the country’s&nbsp;
building codes, for example.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.120
Cities are only permitted to&nbsp;
divide their land into zones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.100
The national government decides what&nbsp;
can and can’t be built in each one.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.140
The United States has no fewer&nbsp;
than twenty-two thousand sets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.560
codes — each one with dozens of different zones.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.520
The entire nation of Japan, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:15.720
is divided into just twelve — total. And any&nbsp;
changes made to one of them applies everywhere.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:23.040
Another seemingly minor difference with major&nbsp;
downstream effects is the way these zones work.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:29.640
While a commercial zone in America typically&nbsp;
allows only commercial activity — things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:34.380
auto repair shops, movie theaters,&nbsp;
and strip malls — the same one in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.720
Japan also allows for non-commercial&nbsp;
uses, like apartments and schools.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:48.360
What this means is that developers are free, if&nbsp;
they want, to mix uses. That free house in Asago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:53.640
for instance, is about 30 feet from a hair&nbsp;
salon and a three-minute walk from a book store.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.300
In Japan, your neighbors really have no&nbsp;
say over what you do with your property — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.900
degree of freedom that results in&nbsp;
some pretty quirky design choices.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.440
On the tame end of the spectrum, there’s&nbsp;
the stuff of HOA nightmares: You might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.420
find a traditional wooden house directly&nbsp;
adjacent to a modern, minimalist mansion.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.240
Then there are the truly creative&nbsp;
ones: the Gothic tomato cottages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.740
transparent townhouses, and giant pyramids.&nbsp;
If you can dream it, it can surely be built.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:33.720
One consequence is that Japan&nbsp;
produces more architects per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:39.660
capita than any other country — and by a&nbsp;
wide margin — 5 times more than the U.K.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.040
7 times more than America,&nbsp;
and 11 times more than Canada.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.140
It’s hard to imagine a better place&nbsp;
for young architects to experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.760
than a nation of tiny homes free from&nbsp;
concerns about “neighborhood character”.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.640
But wait a second. Four times as many new homes as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:05.640
New York? These numbers aren’t just&nbsp;
impressive. They’re… too impressive.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.700
Think about it this way: Here are&nbsp;
seven large, advanced economies.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.880
This is each one’s total population.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:20.460
And this is the number of new homes&nbsp;
each one builds in a typical year.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:29.280
One country clearly stands out. How can it&nbsp;
be that Japan, with only 40% as many people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.360
builds almost as many new homes&nbsp;
each year as the United States?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:41.940
We all know California is starved for housing,&nbsp;
but this is just crazy. The math doesn’t add up.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:49.080
The solution to this puzzle, it turns out,&nbsp;
lies in the number of existing homes sold.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:56.100
Now we can see that although the total number&nbsp;
of homes sold in each country — both new and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:01.920
used — is roughly proportional to its population,&nbsp;
there’s a huge difference in composition.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:06.660
The vast majority of American&nbsp;
home sales are second-hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.040
whereas the vast majority of&nbsp;
Japanese home sales are brand new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.340
In fact, if we stretch the data out, we can&nbsp;
see that it’s almost the inverse of America.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:25.260
And that’s Japan’s secret. The reason its&nbsp;
housing market is so fundamentally different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.580
It’s not just about national control of zoning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.680
it’s also an entirely different&nbsp;
perspective on what housing is.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.860
In most advanced economies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:44.580
a house is an investment that doubles as&nbsp;
a place to live — a 401k you can sleep in.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.680
In Japan, a house is a consumer good — not unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.940
a car or a refrigerator — that&nbsp;
rapidly depreciates in value.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.020
In a mere thirty years, the typical Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.480
home goes from brand-new to about&nbsp;
valuable as a CD player in 2023.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.760
That’s no exaggeration. According&nbsp;
to the Ministry of Land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:14.160
even a reinforced-concrete apartment&nbsp;
has a lifespan of just 37 years.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:21.000
Try to sell your house on the 38th year and&nbsp;
banks will tell you it’s worth zero dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.980
or less since the building has to be&nbsp;
demolished before a new one can be built.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.940
As you can see here, used homes depreciate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:33.840
on average, over twice as&nbsp;
fast in Japan as in the U.S.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.180
One reason for this is relatively&nbsp;
straightforward: earthquakes.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:44.400
Not only do they destroy homes, but they&nbsp;
require periodic updates to building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:50.160
codes to better prepare for the next one.&nbsp;
Newer buildings are considered much safer.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.760
Another reason has to do with history.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.820
Nearly a third of urban Japanese homes&nbsp;
were bombed during the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.000
creating a shortage of 4 million units.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.860
And when private banks were unable&nbsp;
to keep up with demand for mortgages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.080
the government was forced to step in.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.940
Starting in June of 1950, generously subsidized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:18.000
interest rates were offered&nbsp;
to anyone in need of a home.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.300
These millions of dollars of stimulus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:26.820
redirected from the war, helped pay&nbsp;
for nearly 20 million units by 2007.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.800
But this overwhelming success came&nbsp;
with an unintended side-effect.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.980
In the rush to house war-torn Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:40.500
building standards — even those of the&nbsp;
1950s — were haphazardly overlooked.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:47.460
These were cheap, wooden houses with poor&nbsp;
insulation and sometimes major structural flaws.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.620
Because of this, they tended to last&nbsp;
just three or four decades before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:55.980
had to be completely destroyed&nbsp;
and rebuilt from the ground up.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:01.980
The end result was a persistent,&nbsp;
widespread practice that lasts to this day.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:09.360
The quality of today’s homes, of course,&nbsp;
hardly resembles those of 1950. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:15.480
no reason any built this decade, if well cared&nbsp;
for, couldn’t survive well beyond thirty years.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:22.140
But because they’re valued like fragile consumer&nbsp;
goods, owners have little incentive to put in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:27.480
necessary maintenance, turning the 30-year&nbsp;
lifespan into a self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:34.500
Meanwhile, until 2015, homeowners had little&nbsp;
incentive to demolish any houses they weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:40.860
living in thanks to another legacy of World&nbsp;
War II. Also to increase the supply of housing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.960
small residential plots were taxed 80%&nbsp;
less if they contained a structure.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:53.520
That 1,400-square-foot home from earlier would&nbsp;
be taxed at just $75 a year, for example.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:59.460
It’s exactly these kinds of seemingly&nbsp;
minor historical details that shape the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.700
cities we live in today, and in “Great Cities”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:08.400
Dave from City Beautiful explains how,&nbsp;
for Shanghai, Canberra, and Paris.

